  "narrative": "All newly delivered due comparisons have valid Welch results and sufficient cells. Using the run convention, for direction -1 mean differences a positive signed estimate means the exposed group has a more negative dependency score, i.e. stronger dependency; for the direction +1 KEAP1-loss NFE2L2 claim a positive signed estimate means weaker, less negative NFE2L2 dependency. I used approximately 0.10 dependency-score units as the relevance benchmark consistent with the run's 10% decision rule. H247 is accepted: in female lines, PTEN loss is associated with stronger STAT3 dependency (+0.237, 95% CI +0.156 to +0.318). This extends the accepted overall and male-stratified PTEN-loss STAT3 association to females, but it does not by itself establish a statistically supported sex interaction. H249-H252 accept the four core associations in the missing/low-quality matched-omics setting: SMARCA4 loss remains associated with much stronger SMARCA2 dependency, MSI-high with much stronger WRN dependency, KEAP1 loss with much weaker NFE2L2 dependency, and PTEN loss with stronger STAT3 dependency. This is an important technical robustness check because it shows the associations are not confined to QC-passed matched-omics samples, though they remain unadjusted internal associations rather than causal effects. H253-H256 accept robustness in selected co-alteration and culture contexts: PTEN-loss stronger STAT3 dependency among STK11-loss lines, KEAP1-loss weaker NFE2L2 dependency and SMARCA4-loss stronger SMARCA2 dependency among CDKN2A-loss lines, and MSI-high stronger WRN dependency under high-serum conditions. All intervals lie clearly above the relevance threshold and directionally match the core claims. The three new biological hypotheses do not support scientifically meaningful positive dependency associations. H257 is rejected because MYC amplification shows a small opposite-direction signed estimate for WRN dependency (-0.033, CI -0.064 to -0.002), not a clinically meaningful strengthening. H258 is rejected because BRCA2 loss on NFE2L2 dependency is near null with upper bound below threshold (+0.014, CI -0.051 to +0.079). H259 is rejected because ARID1A loss on STAT3 dependency is small and null-leaning (-0.013, CI -0.039 to +0.013). No leakage is apparent; these are measured-predictor to outcome comparisons. Residual confounding remains possible because all estimates are unadjusted and could be affected by library, batch, Cas9 activity, screen QC, ploidy/aneuploidy, mutation burden, subtype, culture, media, and co-alterations. Given multiplicity, only effects with intervals clearly above the relevance benchmark were accepted. Voluntary validation capacity is exhausted, so no validation request is made. H248 is an exact duplicate of H247; I did not create a separate conclusion for it to avoid double-counting the same canonical comparison. I registered low-Cas9-activity mean-difference tests of the four core associations as the next high-value internal follow-up because Cas9 efficiency is a plausible technical modifier not fully covered by prior screen-quality and matched-omics checks.",
  "research_notes": "Iteration 20 appraise. Validation capacity exhausted. Accepted new due claims: H247 female PTEN-loss stronger STAT3 dependency; H249-H252 core associations in missing/low-quality matched-omics setting; H253 PTEN-STAT3 among STK11 loss; H254 KEAP1-NFE2L2 among CDKN2A loss; H255 SMARCA4-SMARCA2 among CDKN2A loss; H256 MSI-WRN in high serum. Rejected H257 MYC-WRN, H258 BRCA2-NFE2L2, H259 ARID1A-STAT3 as not clinically meaningful; H257 is also directionally opposite. H248 duplicate not separately assessed. Core accepted internal associations remain SMARCA4 loss-SMARCA2 dependency, MSI-high-WRN dependency, KEAP1 loss-weaker NFE2L2 dependency, PTEN loss-stronger STAT3 dependency. Next registered analyses test these four core mean differences in low Cas9 activity (cas9_activity_score <= 0.846). All conclusions are unadjusted internal associations in the supplied lung-lineage CRISPR dataset, not causal mechanisms or transportable therapeutic predictions."
